# manifest.tkr — the MANIFEST-STAGE compile-fail channel, plus one positive `Given source` member.
#
# WHAT IT PINS: an unrecognized `[artifact] kind` spelling is a HARD manifest error, and its message
# is USABLE — it names the spelling that was read and enumerates the accepted set. Both properties
# are asserted, because the fix is worth nothing if the message only says "no".
#
# WHY THREE SCENARIOS AND NOT THREE FIXTURES: they are three properties of ONE message emitted by ONE
# failing build of THIS project (a compile-fail scenario declaring no `Given source` is checked
# against the project's own failing build, cached across the whole file — `tkr_compile_fail_artifact`
# → `tkr_ensure_built`). A second bad spelling would re-prove the identical `artifact_from_kind`
# lookup miss and buy nothing but a build; the unit layer (`src/build/manifest_test.tkt`) owns the
# per-spelling table, including `kind = "tool"` and the four accepted kinds still resolving.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT FOLD INTO `diagnostics` OR `parse_diagnostics`: this build fails while READING THE
# `.tkp`, before any `.tks` is lexed. Those two channels reach the parser and the checker only because
# their own manifests parse — a build whose manifest must fail and a build whose manifest must parse
# are mutually exclusive.

Feature: an unknown `[artifact] kind` is a hard manifest error, not a silent Binary

  Scenario: an unrecognized kind spelling fails the build instead of falling back to Binary
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "unknown [artifact] kind"

  Scenario: the rejection names the offending spelling
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "binari"

  Scenario: the rejection enumerates every accepted kind
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "accepted kinds: binary, static, shared, package"

# seed_literal_arm — the behavioral member of the CROSS-ADOPTION of a literal in a `match` arm. It
# rides as a `Given source` case (build STANDALONE, its own synthesized manifest), so it is IMMUNE to
# THIS project's broken `.tkp` (`kind = "binari"`, which fails the PROJECT build above before any
# `.tks` is lexed). It pins that the two spellings — the annotated (`0 to i32`, which the seed accepts)
# and the bare (`0`, which depends on the tip's cross-adoption) — give the SAME value at the SAME
# width: 41 + 0 + 41 + 0 + 41 = 123. If the cross-adoption regressed, the case would stop COMPILING
# ("the function's final expression does not match its declared return type"), the CI diagnostic that
# lost nine artefact legs on the seed. UM SO CENARIO, UMA SO ROTA (native): the standalone build's env
# is per-source, so this positive native member never shares the failing project build's config.
  Scenario: seed_literal_arm — the annotated and adopted spellings of the literal arm give the same 123
    Given source = "cases/seed_literal_arm.tks"
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "123"
